  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Additional Financing-V of the WB to Zimbabwe</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The World Bank Supported Additional Financing V Project Development Objective is to:
A. Increase coverage and quality of an integrated package of Reproductive, Maternal, Neonatal, Child, Adolescent health and nutrition (RMNCAH-N) services, as well as 
B. Strengthen COVID-19 response and institutional capacity to manage performance-based contracts consistent with the MoHCC&apos;s ongoing health initiatives.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The catchment populations of the health facilities contracted (via the PCU or Cordaid) under the different parts of the RBF program and the Urban Voucher program.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Zimbabwe, a landlocked  and lower-middle income  country with a gross national income per capita of US$1,790 (Atlas method, 2018) and a population of 14.4 million (World Development Indicators/WDI 2019), was already facing a deep economic and humanitarian crisis prior to the COVID-19 pandemic.  At the same time Zimbabwe had made commendable improvements in key health outcomes in the past decade, but progress was not fast enough to meet the country&#x2019;s 2015 Millennium Development Goals and current projections fall short of its Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs).</narrative>
  </description>
